@@s001 Writing concurrent tests
@@s002 dato:thread that works,ora:waiting at the barrier,ok:started together with the others
@@s003 The barrier is created with <b>nrThreads + 1</b> participants: the 3 threads of the test plus the <b>main</b>, the thread that runs the test. It opens only when all 4 have arrived.
@@s004 t1 starts at once and reaches the first <code>await()</code>: it stops there.
@@s005 t2 arrives: 2 out of 4. Without the barrier t1 would already have done all its work alone, and the threads would run almost one after the other.
@@s006 t3 arrives: 3 out of 4. One participant is still missing.
@@s007 The main does its <code>await()</code>: 4 out of 4, the barrier opens and the three threads <b>start at the same moment</b>. This is how contention is maximised: more operations in parallel, more chances of a bad interleaving.
@@s008 The same barrier is cyclic: it is reused. At the second <code>await()</code> the main waits for everybody to finish, and only then checks the property with the <b>assertion</b>.
@@s009 dato:main,ora:thread,no:property violated,ok:inside the critical section
@@s010 dato:producers,due:consumers,ok:equal sums,no:different sums
@@s011 Writing concurrent tests
@@s012 Lecture 5 and exercises 5.1–5.2 (part of Assignment 3). A concurrent test cannot choose the interleaving: it can only make the bad one <b>probable</b>. Here the lecture recipe, JUnit, the barrier to start the threads together, and the real results on the sets of the exercise.
@@s013 The recipe in 4 points (slide 25)
@@s014 Before writing code
@@s015 "After N threads have done X inc(), the counter is N·X." It becomes the final assertion.
@@s016 The same test runs on all the implementations (Buggy, Sync, Library): only the line in the <code>@BeforeEach</code> method changes.
@@s017 More threads than cores (on 8 cores, 16 threads) and a <code>CyclicBarrier</code> to start them together.
@@s018 <code>@RepeatedTest(n)</code> and <code>@ParameterizedTest</code>: more executions, more interleavings visited. The exercise says up to 5000–10000 repetitions may be needed.
@@s019 JUnit
@@s020 What it is for
@@s021 <code>@BeforeEach</code>
@@s022 method run before <b>each</b> test: it creates a new object to test
@@s023 <code>@Test</code> / <code>@RepeatedTest(100)</code>
@@s024 one test, run once or 100 times
@@s025 <code>@ParameterizedTest</code> + <code>@MethodSource("argsGeneration")</code>
@@s026 the same test with many parameters (number of threads, of operations), generated by a method that returns a list of <code>Arguments</code>
@@s027 <code>@DisplayName("…")</code>
@@s028 the name shown in the report
@@s029 <code>assertTrue(cond, "message")</code>, <code>assertEquals(expected, value)</code>
@@s030 the property to check
@@s031 <code>gradle cleanTest test --tests exercises05.ConcurrentSetTest</code>
@@s032 runs the tests; the HTML report is in <code>build/reports/tests/test/</code>
@@s033 CyclicBarrier(nrThreads + 1)
@@s034 The barrier starts the threads almost one at a time
@@s035 Inside, <code>CyclicBarrier</code> uses a lock (<code>ReentrantLock</code>) and a condition. When the last one arrives, it wakes everybody, but every woken thread must <b>take that lock again</b> before leaving <code>await()</code>: they leave in a queue. If every thread does one single, very short operation, the first ones have already finished when the last ones leave, and the collisions are rare.
@@s036 Measured with the <code>push</code> test of exercise 7.2.2 (1000 threads, one <code>push</code> each) against a <code>push</code> deliberately wrong (<code>get</code> and then <code>set</code>): with <code>CyclicBarrier</code> the error comes out in <b>1 repetition out of 20</b>. With a <code>CountDownLatch(1)</code> as "starting gun" (all do <code>await()</code>, the main does <code>countDown()</code> once) and <code>join()</code> for the end: <b>18 out of 20</b>. The latch wakes the threads without making them pass through an exclusive lock. The <code>CountDownLatch</code> is not in the course slides: the barrier remains the "official" technique, the latch is a reinforcement.
@@s037 Exercise 5.1: the sets
@@s038 Three implementations of <code>ConcurrentIntegerSet</code>: <b>Buggy</b> (a <code>HashSet</code> without protection), <b>Sync</b> (to correct) and <b>Library</b> (<code>ConcurrentSkipListSet</code>). The test: 16 threads all add the same numbers 0…999, so at the end the size must be <b>1000</b>; then, with the numbers already inside, they remove them all: the size must be <b>0</b>.
@@s039 Implementation
@@s040 add: failed tests
@@s041 remove: failed tests
@@s042 Buggy (HashSet)
@@s043 <b>169 / 200</b> (size up to 1633)
@@s044 <b>23 / 200</b> (size down to −219)
@@s045 Sync (<code>synchronized</code> methods)
@@s046 Library (ConcurrentSkipListSet)
@@s047 Run with JDK 26 on a 12-core machine: 16 threads, CyclicBarrier, 200 repetitions (a program with the same structure as the JUnit test). A size of 1633 for a set of 1000 numbers, or a negative one, is impossible in a sequential execution.
@@s048 Build the interleaving of add(5) yourself
@@s049 Two threads call <code>add(5)</code> on an empty set. <code>HashSet.add</code> is not atomic: here it is split into three steps (look, insert the node, increment size). Find an interleaving that leaves 5 in the set <b>once</b> but with <b>size = 2</b>.
@@s050 Two add(5) in parallel
@@s051 5.1.3: why synchronized repairs it
@@s052 With <code>add</code>, <code>remove</code> and <code>size</code> all <code>synchronized</code> on the same object, the three steps of one operation can no longer be mixed with those of another (mutual exclusion), and each thread sees the writes of the previous one (happens-before of the unlock on the next lock, <a href="l03-happens-before.html">lecture 3</a>). The internal set is confined: it does not leave the class (<a href="l04-confinamento.html">confinement</a>).
@@s053 5.1.5 and 5.1.6: what a test proves
@@s054 The failure is a concrete counterexample: a concurrent execution that produces a result impossible in sequence. With the course definition (no data race on the fields) the reasoning is that of your answer: a wrong size can arise only from unsynchronised concurrent accesses to the fields of HashSet.
@@s055 Only some interleavings have been visited, and only the final size has been checked. Dijkstra: tests show the presence of bugs, not their absence.
@@s056 Exercise 5.2: the semaphore
@@s057 Capacity 2, yet 3 threads in the critical section (verified: 3 executions out of 3)
@@s058 <code>acquire()</code> waits only if state == capacity; <code>release()</code> does state-- without checking anything.
@@s059 Nobody had done acquire: it is like giving away an extra permit. The main too is part of the interleaving.
@@s060 When each checks, state is −1, 0, 1: never equal to 2, so nobody waits.
@@s061 Herlihy's property violated. Your answer 5.2.1 is this one, and it is right. The test does not depend on the scheduling: an ordinary <code>@Test</code> is enough.
@@s062 Three threads inside with capacity 2
@@s063 The notation m(2) → t1(1) → t2(1) → t3(1)
@@s064 The numbers are the labels of the comments in <code>SemaphoreImp.java</code>: <code>// [1]</code> is above <code>acquire()</code>, <code>// [2]</code> above <code>release()</code>. So <code>m(2)</code> = the <b>main</b> calls <code>release()</code>, <code>t1(1)</code> = the thread t1 calls <code>acquire()</code>. The arrow → means "and then". The interleaving is written at the level of the <b>method calls</b> because the fields are <code>private</code>: from outside they cannot be touched (Hint I of the exercise).
@@s065 5.2.2: the capacity() test (SemaphoreImpTest.java, shortened)
@@s066 The test rebuilds the interleaving of 5.2.1: CAPACITY = 2, THREADS_NUMBER = CAPACITY + 1 = 3. The new semaphore is created in <code>@BeforeEach</code>.
@@s067 The barrier has <b>4</b> participants: the 3 threads plus the main. It opens only when all 4 have arrived.
@@s068 The counter of "how many have entered". It is an <code>AtomicInteger</code> because 3 threads increment it together: with an <code>int</code> the test itself would have a data race.
@@s069 It is the step <code>m(2)</code>: the main calls <code>release()</code> <b>before</b> creating the threads. state goes from 0 to −1: the extra permit is already there when the threads start.
@@s070 It creates and starts 3 threads. <code>() -&gt; { … }</code> is a lambda: the body of the <code>run()</code> method of the thread.
@@s071 First barrier: the 3 threads and the main wait for each other, then start together.
@@s072 It is the step <code>t(1)</code>: each thread does <code>acquire()</code>. If it does not block, for the exercise it <b>has entered</b> the critical section, and counts it. Nobody calls <code>release()</code>: whoever enters stays inside.
@@s073 Second barrier: the main waits for everybody to have counted. Only afterwards it reads the counter.
@@s074 Herlihy's property: at most CAPACITY threads inside. With the faulty semaphore the value is always 3 and the test <b>always fails</b>.
@@s075 Why <code>@Test</code> is enough and not <code>@RepeatedTest</code>: after <code>m(2)</code> state is −1, and each <code>acquire()</code> raises it by 1 (−1 → 0 → 1 → 2). Whichever thread arrives first always finds state &lt; 2. The order of the threads does not change the result.
@@s076 With a correct semaphore this test does not pass: it blocks
@@s077 If the semaphore were correct, the third thread would stay stopped in <code>acquire()</code> and would never reach the second <code>barrier.await()</code>. The barrier waits for 4 participants, only 3 arrive: the main stays blocked and the test does not finish. A test should be able to <b>pass</b> on a correct implementation. For example, in the main one can use <code>barrier.await(1, TimeUnit.SECONDS)</code> and handle the <code>TimeoutException</code>; after the timeout the counter is 2 and the assertion is true.
@@s078 The bounded buffer of the lecture
@@s079 Property: if producers and consumers put and take the same number of elements, <b>the sum of the numbers put is equal to the sum of the numbers taken</b>. Each producer accumulates its local sum and at the end adds it to a global <code>AtomicInteger</code> (<code>putSum</code>); the consumers do the same with <code>takeSum</code>; at the end <code>assert(putSum.get() == takeSum.get())</code>. The test trusts <code>AtomicInteger</code>: if the object used to check were wrong, the test would be worth nothing.
@@s080 Challenge 5.1.7–5.1.8: size() can lie
@@s081 In <code>HashMap</code> size() reads a field, updated after the insertion of the node: a thread that calls size() between the insertion and the size++ of another thread sees an old value. For <code>ConcurrentSkipListSet</code> the documentation says that size() traverses the structure and can be inexact if the set changes during the count. Testing it is difficult: one would need to know how many elements there really are at the exact instant in which size() reads, and a test cannot stop all the threads at that point.
@@s082 putSum == takeSum
